<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=radar">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as RADAR</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Radar is a system that uses radio waves to find the distance, direction and speed of objects such as aircraft, ships and weather formations; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> coined the name in 1940 from radio detection and ranging. In 1904 the German inventor Christian Hülsmeyer detected a ship in dense fog with his telemobiloscope, but German military officials rejected it. Pulsed radar was first shown in December 1934 by Robert M. Page at the Naval Research Laboratory, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> Robert Watson-Watt's team built the Chain Home stations, operational from 1936, which helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> win the Battle of Britain. The cavity magnetron made small sets possible.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/radar.jpg" alt="Radar" width="330" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: US Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radar_antenna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1904</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Christian Hülsmeyer · Robert M. Page · Robert Watson-Watt</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Radar was developed in secret by several countries before and during World War II; Heinrich Hertz had shown in 1886 that radio waves reflect from solid objects, and the first pulsed systems were shown in the United States in 1934 and in Germany and Great Britain in 1935.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/radio">Radio<span>1895</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=radar">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
